devto_markdown:
In many backend microservices, configuration maps, static lookup tables, and feature flags are initialized once at startup and read millions of times throughout the application lifecycle. Using a standard `System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary<TKey, TValue>` for these scenarios is a hidden tax on your memory allocations and Gen0 garbage collection cycles.

### Before
```csharp
private readonly Dictionary<string, int> _settings = new()
{
    {"Timeout", 30},
    {"Retries", 3},
    {"MaxBatch", 500}
};

public int Get(string key) => _settings[key];
```

Standard dictionaries must handle dynamic resizing, collision chains, and structural modifications. Even when your data is strictly read-only after startup, the runtime still carries the overhead of mutable structures.

### After
```csharp
private readonly FrozenDictionary<string, int> _settings = new Dictionary<string, int>
{
    {"Timeout", 30},
    {"Retries", 3},
    {"MaxBatch", 500}
}.ToFrozenDictionary();

public int Get(string key) => _settings[key];
```

### Why it matters
- **Zero Allocations:** `FrozenDictionary<TKey, TValue>` optimizes the internal bucket and entry layout for read-only workloads, dropping query heap allocations to zero bytes.
- **Performance Boost:** Benchmarks show read throughput beats standard dictionaries by up to 40% due to superior CPU cache utilization and flattened array traversal.
- **Initialization Cost:** Freezing the dictionary is an expensive operation. Always perform `.ToFrozenDictionary()` during application startup or type construction, never per request.

### Watch out
Never use frozen collections for data that changes dynamically. Mutation requires calling `ToFrozenDictionary()` again, which completely rebuilds the underlying hash structures and negates any performance gains.

Where are you still using mutable collections for static reference data?
